var CT={"pillar": {"label": "PILLAR ARTICLE · HOMEOWNER GUIDE", "title": "Who Works For You", "intro": ["When damage hits your home in {{CITY}}, whether it's a storm, a burst pipe, a fire or a slow leak you just found, your insurance company opens a file and puts a team of professionals to work right away. Most homeowners assume the person who comes out to inspect is there to make sure they get paid fairly, but that person works for the carrier, and you have the legal right to bring in your own licensed representative. Do you know who is actually looking out for your side of the claim before you sign anything?", "Filing a claim feels like a simple paperwork process until the first repair estimate comes back way lower than what a local contractor charges. You want to trust that your policy will cover the damage you paid premiums to protect, but insurance contracts are written in dense language that makes it easy to miss money you are owed unless someone reads every page for you. Have you had a chance to look at your policy's actual replacement rules, or would you like me to walk through them with you?"], "quote": "Your insurance company has a full team protecting their bottom line, and you deserve someone protecting yours.", "lead": "The moment you call in a loss and get a claim number, your insurance company puts four different layers of people on your file. You only ever see one of them in person, but knowing how the other three operate behind the scenes helps you understand why claims get delayed or underpaid. Would you like to know what happens to your file after the inspection truck leaves your driveway?", "team": [["The field adjuster", "The one you meet", "They are often an independent contractor hired for the day who may work for several different insurance companies at once. They are usually friendly and polite while measuring and taking photos, but they do not have the authority to decide what you get paid. Have you already had someone walk your property, or are you still waiting for that first visit?"], ["The desk adjuster", "The one you never meet", "They read your claim notes first, tell the field adjuster what to look at, often just one area like the roof or the room with the stain, so nobody checks the rest of the house, and review the final report line by line. But you can submit your own complete proof of all the damage so nothing gets left off the table. Did the person who inspected your home look at everything, or only one spot?"], ["The supervisor", "Watches the numbers", "The manager who watches the claim numbers and keeps total payouts in line. A desk adjuster's authority can stop at a certain dollar limit, but a documented, line-by-line estimate backed by your policy language can push your claim to a proper review. Are you worried your claim is stuck waiting on someone higher up?"], ["The legal department", "Waiting in the background", "As long as a homeowner accepts the first check and nobody pushes back on a low estimate or a partial denial, the lawyers never have to get involved. But you do not have to accept an unfair settlement just to avoid a fight when a licensed advocate can negotiate the numbers for you."]], "close_h": "Four people work for your insurance company. Who works for you?", "close": "That's my job. I work only for you. I inspect the whole house, inside and out, build my own estimate, and handle the back-and-forth with your insurance company. Are you ready to talk about getting someone in your corner?", "why": "I became a licensed public adjuster in {{LICENSED_SINCE}} because I saw too many good families in {{CITY}} accept low payouts simply because they did not know how the claim system works. Insurance policies are complicated, but when someone explains your rights in plain English and documents every inch of damage, the playing field evens out. Ready to tell me what happened to your home?", "steps": [["Free claim review", "We talk about what happened to your home, look at your policy and any letters you already got from the insurer, and I give you an honest look at where your claim stands, at no cost."], ["Full property inspection", "I come out to your home, inspect the whole property, roof, walls, ceilings, floors and hidden areas, and document every bit of damage the carrier may have missed."], ["Estimate and negotiation", "I put together a detailed repair estimate and handle the calls, emails and paperwork with your insurance company so you don't have to."]]}, "faq": [["Why is my first insurance check way smaller than my roofer's estimate?", "Insurance companies usually pay claims in two parts, starting with the depreciated value of your damaged property rather than the full replacement cost. They can also leave out local building code upgrades, labor rates, or hidden water damage that a contractor sees right away. But you may be able to recover the held-back money and add missed items once the full scope of repairs is properly documented and submitted.", "I review your insurer's payout sheet side by side with real local repair costs to find what they left out.", "Want to send over your insurance estimate so we can see why the numbers are so far apart?", "first-check-smaller"], ["The adjuster only spent ten minutes at my house and looked at one area. Is that normal?", "Field adjusters often carry heavy caseloads and may only have instructions from the desk adjuster to look at one specific area. When they rush through and skip the attic, walls, floors or ceilings, hidden water and other damage easily go unrecorded. But an incomplete inspection does not close the door on your claim when you bring in thorough photo and moisture documentation.", "I walk your whole home, inside and out, and document what the first inspection missed.", "Would you like me to come out and do a full top-to-bottom check?", "adjuster-only-roof"], ["Should I cash the first check the insurance company sent me?", "Many homeowners freeze when a check arrives because they worry that depositing it means the claim is finished forever. In many cases, cashing an initial undisputed payment does not stop you from asking for the rest of what you are owed, but always check the memo line and the letter that came with it to make sure it does not say \"full and final settlement.\"", "I look at the paperwork that came with your check before you endorse it, so you know what rights you keep.", "Did a letter come with that check, and would you like me to look it over with you?", "cash-the-check"], ["My claim was denied for \"wear and tear\" or a \"long-term leak.\" Can I still fight it?", "Insurers frequently blame roof leaks, pipe leaks and water damage on age, wear and tear, or a leak they say went on too long. A denial letter feels like the final word, but sudden damage leaves different signs than slow aging when it's documented correctly.", "I inspect denied claims for the real cause of the damage and build a factual response for your carrier.", "When did you get your denial letter, and can we talk about what the damage actually looks like?", "wear-and-tear-denial"], ["How much does it cost to hire a public adjuster in Florida?", "Public adjusters usually work on a contingency fee, which means you pay nothing upfront and pay a percentage of the claim money recovered. Fees vary by claim and by whether the state has declared an emergency, but Florida law caps what we can charge and requires that you get the fee in writing before signing any contract.", "I go over my written fee agreement line by line with you first so there are no surprises.", "Would you like to talk through how the fee works on a claim like yours before you decide?", "public-adjuster-cost-florida"], ["Will my insurance company drop me or raise my rates if I hire a public adjuster?", "Florida homeowners worry about losing coverage in a tough insurance market, and carriers do adjust rates after heavy loss years. Rate and renewal decisions depend on your insurer and your policy, but hiring a licensed public adjuster is your right, and your agent can tell you how a claim affects your policy.", "I handle the claim professionally and keep your repair timeline on track.", "Are you holding back on getting help because you're worried how your carrier will react?", "will-insurer-drop-me"], ["Should I make repairs before the insurance adjuster comes to look at my house?", "You have a duty under your policy to protect your home from further damage, like tarping a leaking roof or extracting standing water. Tearing out drywall or replacing a roof before the insurer sees it can hurt your claim, but clear photos and videos taken before emergency dry-out protect both your house and your claim.", "I tell you what temporary steps to take right now and how to document everything before permanent work starts.", "Have you already started emergency repairs, or are you figuring out what to do first?", "repairs-before-adjuster"], ["What is \"recoverable depreciation\" and how do I get it back?", "When an insurer calculates your loss, it subtracts a percentage for the age and condition of your roof or flooring and holds that money back until the work is done. That held-back amount is called recoverable depreciation, but if you have a Replacement Cost Value policy, you can claim it once you show proof that repairs are underway or done.", "I track your depreciation holdbacks and submit the right invoices so the carrier releases the rest.", "Do you see a depreciation line on your claim summary that hasn't been paid yet?", "recoverable-depreciation"], ["Can't my roofer or general contractor just handle the insurance claim for me?", "Good contractors know how to rebuild your house and can give you a fair price for their trade. Under Florida law, a contractor cannot negotiate your insurance settlement unless they also hold a public adjuster license, but working alongside a public adjuster lets your contractor focus on building while I handle the policy and pricing.", "I coordinate directly with your contractor so the approved scope matches what it takes to fix your home.", "Has a contractor already given you a quote your insurance company is pushing back on?", "roofer-handle-claim"], ["How long do I have to file or reopen a claim in Florida?", "Florida has strict deadlines for reporting a new claim and shorter windows for a supplemental claim when hidden damage shows up later. Waiting too long can bar you from collecting money you are owed, but acting quickly preserves your right to reopen an underpaid file.", "I check your date of loss against current Florida deadlines right away.", "When did the damage happen, and would you like to check how much time you have left?", "claim-deadlines-florida"]], "articles": [{"slug": "first-check-smaller", "title": "Why Is My First Insurance Check Smaller Than My Estimate?", "captures": "Why is my insurance payout so low compared to my contractor estimate?", "short": "Your first insurance check is usually smaller because the carrier subtracts depreciation for the age of what was damaged and can leave out hidden repairs or local code upgrades. Under a replacement cost policy, this first check is rarely the final amount owed, but you have to document the missing items and request the rest properly. Have you looked at the line-by-line breakdown that came with your payment yet?", "body": ["Opening an envelope from your insurance company and seeing a check for half of what your contractor quoted is a sinking feeling. Most homeowners assume the insurer simply refused to pay for their repairs, but the gap usually comes down to how insurance math works and what the first inspector left off the list. Would you like to know the reasons that check came up short?", "The biggest reason is called depreciation, which is how insurers reduce the value of your roof, cabinets, drywall or flooring based on how old they are. If what was damaged is twelve years old, the company may hold back thousands of dollars from the first check until you prove the repairs are being made, but if you have a Replacement Cost Value policy, that held-back money still belongs to you once the proper paperwork is filed. Do you know whether your policy pays Replacement Cost or Actual Cash Value?", "The second reason is that the first scope of work often misses real construction steps. An estimate may only pay to patch one section of a roof, replace a strip of flooring or paint one wall, ignoring that old materials break when you touch them and new ones won't match across an open room, but showing why a partial repair won't work makes the carrier look at the full repair area. Did your insurer's estimate only pay to patch a small section?", "The third reason is hidden damage that nobody looked for during a quick walkthrough. Water travels behind baseboards, under laminate flooring and through attic insulation long before a brown stain shows on your ceiling, but a thorough moisture check and full inspection can bring those unseen repairs into your claim. Has anyone checked behind your walls or inside your attic since the damage happened?", "Many people also forget the deductible when they look at the bottom line. If your loss came from a hurricane, Florida hurricane deductibles are often two to five percent of your home's insured value, and even a regular deductible takes a bite out of the first payout, but when the total damage is undercounted, your deductible can eat almost the whole check. Is your deductible taking up most of the money they sent you?", "Finally, estimating software can use pricing that doesn't reflect what licensed tradespeople in {{CITY}} actually charge when demand is high. Labor and material costs jump when everyone in town needs the same repairs at the same time, but accurate local repair bids and code requirements help bring the numbers closer to reality. Have you compared the prices on your insurance sheet against a real local bid?"], "quote": "The first check is an opening offer based on a quick look, not the final word on your home.", "next": ["Print the full Statement of Loss and repair estimate, not just the cover letter with the check.", "Ask a licensed local contractor for a detailed, room-by-room estimate to compare against the insurer's list.", "Find the column labeled \"Depreciation\" to see how much money is being held back.", "Photograph any damage that isn't on the adjuster's report: cracked tiles, warped floors, swollen cabinets, water spots.", "Call a licensed public adjuster to review both estimates side by side before you sign repair contracts."], "close": "You should not have to drain your savings to cover repairs your insurance policy was supposed to pay for. Many homeowners accept the first check and move on without asking questions, but a careful review of your estimate can uncover repairs and held-back funds that were left out. Want to call or text me, {{FIRST}}, at {{PHONE}} so we can go over your payout sheet together?"}, {"slug": "cash-the-check", "title": "Should I Cash My Insurance Check If It Is Too Low?", "captures": "Should I cash the first check from my insurance company?", "short": "In many cases, cashing a first check for undisputed damages does not stop you from asking for the rest of your claim money. Read the check and any letter with it first to make sure it doesn't say \"full and final settlement,\" but a second set of eyes on the paperwork protects your right to supplement the claim. Have you already deposited the check, or is it still sitting on your kitchen counter?", "body": ["When a check shows up for a fraction of your repair bill, it's smart to pause before walking into the bank. Homeowners hear stories about people signing away their rights by endorsing a low settlement check, but knowing what to look for removes the guesswork so you can get repairs started safely. Would you like to know which words on a check should make you stop?", "Insurance companies generally pay the undisputed amount they agree they owe. Cashing that check gives you money to tarp, dry out or order materials while the rest of the claim is negotiated, but if the memo line or letter says cashing it settles the entire claim, depositing it can lock you out of future payments. Have you checked the front and back of the check for release language?", "Another wrinkle comes when you still have a mortgage on your home in {{CITY}}. Your mortgage company's name will almost always be on the check next to yours, so you can't cash it without their endorsement, but knowing their inspection and draw rules ahead of time keeps your money from getting stuck in red tape. Is your mortgage lender listed on the check?", "Some homeowners hold onto a low check for months, thinking it sends a message. Leaving a check in a drawer doesn't make the carrier raise its offer, and checks can expire after ninety or one hundred eighty days, but depositing an undisputed payment while telling the carrier in writing that you dispute the balance keeps your money working for you. Are you worried that cashing it tells the insurer you agree with their number?", "If your check came without a detailed breakdown, don't rush to spend it before seeing the math. You have the right to see every room, measurement and price the desk adjuster used, but once you have the full estimate, you can deposit the undisputed portion and submit a supplemental claim for everything they missed. Did the insurance company send you the full line-by-line estimate?"], "quote": "Cashing an undisputed check starts your recovery. Signing a final release ends your claim.", "next": ["Read the memo line, the back of the check, and every page of the letter in the envelope.", "Look for phrases like \"full and final payment,\" \"release of all claims\" or \"accord and satisfaction\" before signing.", "Keep the check stub and take a clear photo of the check for your claim folder.", "If your mortgage company is on the check, call its loss draft department to learn the endorsement steps.", "Have a licensed public adjuster review the Statement of Loss so you can file a supplement for the unpaid balance."], "close": "A small first check doesn't mean your claim is over, as long as you handle the paperwork carefully from day one. Insurance companies issue partial payments every day, and many homeowners are too nervous or confused to ask for the rest, but you can use that first check to protect your home while I work for the full amount owed under your policy. Ready to call or text {{FIRST}} at {{PHONE}} so we can check that letter together before you go to the bank?"}, {"slug": "adjuster-only-roof", "title": "What To Do When the Adjuster Only Looked at Part of the Damage", "captures": "The insurance adjuster only looked at one area and ignored the rest of the damage. What do I do?", "short": "When an adjuster only inspects one area, like the roof or the room with the stain, your estimate can miss hidden water damage, wet insulation and damage in the rooms next door. Field adjusters often follow narrow instructions from a desk adjuster, but you can request a re-inspection and submit full documentation to get every damaged area included. Did the adjuster who visited look at your whole home?", "body": ["It happens on claims every week in {{CITY}}: someone measures one spot for ten minutes and drives away without looking at the rest of the house. Homeowners feel brushed off when an inspector ignores most of the damage, but understanding why they skip it shows you how to get those areas added to your claim. Would you like to know why the inspection was so narrow?", "Many field adjusters are independent contractors given instructions from the desk adjuster before they arrive. If your first phone report only mentioned the roof or one room, the inspection may have been set up for that area only, but water travels, and damage almost never stays where it started. Have you noticed musty smells, bubbled paint, soft flooring or faint yellow rings since the damage happened?", "Even when water hasn't shown up on a ceiling or wall yet, wet insulation, subfloors and cabinets cause serious problems in Florida's heat and humidity. Hidden moisture invites mold and wood rot, but documenting it shows the damage goes deeper than what the first inspector saw. Has anyone checked inside your attic, walls or cabinets since this started?", "Water travels along rafters, pipes and subfloors, so a leak in one room can show up as warped flooring or swollen baseboards two rooms away. A ten-minute look at one spot will never connect those dots, but a complete room-by-room inspection ties all of the damage back to its source. Are you seeing water spots or warped trim in rooms that seem unrelated?", "If you already got an estimate that only lists one area, your claim file is incomplete. Insurance companies won't pay to replace flooring, dry out walls or rebuild cabinets unless someone puts those measurements and photos in front of the desk adjuster, but a complete scope gives them what they need to reopen the file and account for the whole house. Would you like help documenting the rest of your home before the damage gets worse?"], "quote": "Damage rarely stays where it started, and your inspection shouldn't either.", "next": ["Walk through every room in daylight and look closely at ceilings, floors, baseboards, cabinets and closets.", "Take clear, well-lit photos and videos of every stain, crack, soft spot or swollen baseboard you find.", "If it's safe, check your attic, under sinks and inside cabinets for damp spots or dark water trails.", "Write down the date you first noticed each spot so your file has a clear timeline.", "Call a licensed public adjuster for a full top-to-bottom inspection."], "close": "You pay for coverage on your entire home, not just the one spot someone looked at. Letting an incomplete inspection stand can leave you paying out of pocket for repairs months from now, but a thorough inspection puts the full truth of your loss on the record. Want to call or text {{FIRST}} at {{PHONE}} to schedule a complete walkthrough of your home?"}, {"slug": "wear-and-tear-denial", "title": "My Claim Was Denied for Wear and Tear. Now What?", "captures": "How do I fight a homeowners insurance denial for wear and tear or a long-term leak?", "short": "A denial letter blaming \"wear and tear,\" age or a \"long-term leak\" is one of the most common answers Florida homeowners get, on roofs, plumbing and water damage alike. Insurance companies sometimes blame age when the real damage is subtle, but a denial letter is not the final word if a proper inspection shows something sudden caused it. Did your denial letter say your home simply wore out?", "body": ["Getting a denial letter after years of paying premiums on time feels like a slap in the face. The letter usually quotes policy exclusions and says the damage is normal aging or a slow leak, but sudden damage and long-term wear leave different signs when someone knows what to look for. Would you like to know how the two are told apart?", "Every home in {{CITY}} ages, and older roofs, pipes and fixtures show wear. Sudden damage looks different: a roof lifts and creases in a wind event, a supply line bursts, a drain backs up, and water spreads in a pattern that slow seepage doesn't make, but a rushed inspection can miss those signs until someone documents them carefully. Did the adjuster explain exactly what they saw that made them call it wear and tear?", "Another reason insurers deny older homes is that old materials break when someone tries to repair them. When a repair attempt damages the surrounding area, building rules can require replacing more than a patch, but carriers sometimes call that brittleness wear and tear. Has a contractor told you the damaged area is too old to patch?", "Many homeowners put the denial letter in a drawer and start looking at loans to pay for repairs themselves. Walking away without a second opinion means paying for it all yourself, but having a licensed public adjuster re-inspect the damage and respond with clear evidence can give the carrier a reason to take a second look. Are you about to pay out of pocket just because the first letter said no?", "Time matters once you get a denial, because the damage can keep getting worse and Florida sets deadlines for disputing a claim decision. Leaving it alone can lead to new damage the insurer may blame on neglect, but acting quickly lets you protect your home and dispute the decision while the evidence is still fresh. When did you get your denial letter, and have you stopped the damage from spreading?"], "quote": "Old doesn't mean undamaged, and a denial letter is not the final word.", "next": ["Save the denial letter and its envelope, and write down the date you received it.", "Ask your insurance company for the field adjuster's photos and inspection report.", "Stop the damage from spreading, like a tarp, a shut-off valve or drying fans, and keep every receipt.", "Don't let anyone tear out the damaged area before you get a second inspection.", "Have a licensed public adjuster inspect the damage for its real cause."], "close": "Many homeowners give up the moment they read a denial letter. You don't have to take one adjuster's opinion as the final truth when your own eyes tell you something sudden happened, but you need clear, documented proof to make the carrier take a second look. Ready to call or text {{FIRST}} at {{PHONE}} so we can review your denial letter together?"}, {"slug": "public-adjuster-cost-florida", "title": "How Much Does a Public Adjuster Cost in Florida?", "captures": "How much does it cost to hire a public adjuster in Florida?", "short": "Most public adjusters in Florida work on contingency, so you pay nothing upfront and the fee is a percentage of the claim money recovered. Florida law caps that percentage: 10 percent on claims from a declared state of emergency during the year after the declaration, and 20 percent on other claims, with the fee spelled out in a written contract you can cancel within the period the law allows. Do you know whether your damage came from a declared emergency like a named storm?", "body": ["Most homeowners never ask what a public adjuster costs until they're already worried about money. That makes sense after a loss, when every dollar feels spoken for, but the fee is set by law and written down before any work starts, so nothing about it has to be a mystery. Would it help to see exactly how the percentage is figured?", "A public adjuster in Florida is paid from the claim money the insurance company pays, not from your pocket today. If the insurer pays nothing, a contingency fee is a percentage of nothing, but if the work brings in more money, the fee comes out of that larger amount and you still keep the larger share. Have you already received any payment on this claim?", "Florida caps the fee. For claims from an event the Governor declared a state of emergency, the cap is 10 percent for claims made during the year after the declaration, and for other claims the cap is 20 percent. Some adjusters charge less than the cap, but no licensed public adjuster in Florida can charge more, and the percentage has to be in the written contract you sign. Was your damage from a named storm or another declared emergency?", "Another rule protects homeowners who already got a check. If you hire a public adjuster to reopen or supplement a claim that was partly paid, the fee can only be charged on the new money recovered, not on what the insurer already paid you. That means hiring help late doesn't cost you a cut of the first check, but it does mean the sooner the full damage is documented, the more of the work happens on the first pass. Has your insurer already sent you a payment you think is too low?", "Florida contracts for public adjusters must also tell you how to cancel. You get a window to cancel without penalty after signing, and the contract has to spell it out in large bold type, but the best protection is still reading the fee line with the adjuster before you sign anything. Would you like to go through a fee agreement line by line before you decide?"], "quote": "You should know the fee before you sign, not after the check arrives.", "next": ["Ask any public adjuster for the exact percentage in writing before signing.", "Check whether your loss is tied to a declared state of emergency.", "If you already received a payment, confirm the fee applies only to new money recovered.", "Read the cancellation section of the contract and note the deadline.", "Verify the adjuster's Florida license on the Department of Financial Services website."], "close": "Paying for help after a loss can feel like one more bill you don't need. Many homeowners settle for less because they assume a public adjuster costs more than they'll recover, but you can see the math up front, in writing, before you commit to anything. Ready to call or text {{FIRST}} at {{PHONE}} and talk through how the fee would work on your claim?"}, {"slug": "will-insurer-drop-me", "title": "Will My Insurance Company Drop Me If I Hire a Public Adjuster?", "captures": "Will my insurance company drop me or raise my rates if I hire a public adjuster?", "short": "Hiring a public adjuster is not, by itself, a reason an insurer can give for dropping you. Rates and renewals are driven by the loss, the condition of the home and the insurer's own underwriting, not by who helps you present the claim, and Florida limits when an insurer can cancel or nonrenew a home damaged by a covered peril before repairs are done. Is the worry about this claim, or about what happens at your next renewal?", "body": ["A lot of homeowners hesitate to get help because they're afraid the insurance company will punish them for it. That fear is understandable when you've heard stories of policies not being renewed, but the claim itself is what the insurer looks at, not whether you had someone in your corner presenting it. Would it ease your mind to know what actually drives a nonrenewal?", "Insurers price and renew policies based on risk: the age and condition of the roof, past losses, the location and their own business decisions. Filing a claim can affect your future premium whether or not you hire anyone, but having a licensed professional document the damage accurately doesn't add a second strike to your record. Have you already reported the claim to your insurance company?", "Florida also has rules about canceling a damaged home. In general, an insurer can't cancel or nonrenew a policy on a home damaged by a covered peril until a period after repairs are completed, with limited exceptions. The details depend on your policy and the situation, but the law is written to keep families from losing coverage in the middle of a claim. Are repairs on your home finished, or still in progress?", "Some homeowners try to keep the claim small, hoping it keeps the insurer happy. Leaving damage out doesn't protect your renewal, and missed damage often turns into a bigger problem later, but documenting everything the first time gives you the money to fix the home properly, which is what makes it insurable going forward. Did the insurer's adjuster look at every damaged area?", "If you're worried about the market overall, that's a fair concern in Florida right now. Premiums have risen for many homeowners regardless of their claims, but you can ask your agent directly what factors affect your renewal and keep your home documented and maintained so it stays in good standing. Would it help to talk through what's in your policy before you decide?"], "quote": "Your claim is about your damage. Who helps you present it isn't the risk.", "next": ["Report the claim to your insurer if you haven't already.", "Ask your agent in writing which factors affect your renewal.", "Keep photos, receipts and repair records in one folder.", "Finish repairs promptly once the claim is paid, and keep the invoices.", "Have a licensed public adjuster review your policy before you settle."], "close": "Fear of losing coverage keeps a lot of families from asking for what they're owed. Insurers count on that hesitation, but you can protect your home and your claim at the same time by getting the damage documented right. Ready to call or text {{FIRST}} at {{PHONE}} to talk it through before you decide anything?"}, {"slug": "repairs-before-adjuster", "title": "Should I Make Repairs Before the Insurance Adjuster Comes?", "captures": "Should I make repairs before the insurance adjuster comes to look at my house?", "short": "Make temporary repairs that stop more damage, like tarping a roof, shutting off water or drying out wet areas, and keep every receipt. Hold off on permanent repairs until the damage is documented and the insurer has had its chance to inspect, unless waiting would make things unsafe or worse. Is water still getting in, or is the damage stable for now?", "body": ["Waiting for an adjuster while your house is open to the weather is stressful. Most policies actually require you to protect the property from further damage, but there's a big difference between stopping the damage and fixing it for good, and getting that line right protects your claim. Would it help to know which repairs are safe to do right now?", "Temporary repairs are the ones that keep things from getting worse: a tarp on the roof, plywood over a broken window, shutting off the main water valve, pulling out soaked carpet and running fans. Your policy usually expects these, and the reasonable cost is typically part of the claim, but you need photos before and after and every receipt to get paid for them. Have you taken photos of the damage before touching anything?", "Permanent repairs are different. Replacing the roof, the drywall or the flooring before the insurer inspects can make it harder to prove what was damaged and how badly, but documenting thoroughly first, with photos, video and measurements, keeps the evidence even after the repair. Has the insurer told you when its adjuster will come out?", "Mold is the reason not to wait too long on water damage. Wet drywall and insulation can start growing mold within days in Florida's heat, so drying out and removing soaked materials is part of protecting the home, but keep a sample piece and photos of what you removed so the damage is still on record. Is anything in the house still wet?", "If the insurer is slow to inspect and you can't safely wait, write down that you asked and when. Florida requires insurers to acknowledge and act on claims within set timeframes, but a written record of your requests protects you if they later question why you repaired before they came. Would you like help putting together a damage folder today?"], "quote": "Stop the damage now. Prove the damage before you fix it for good.", "next": ["Photograph and video every damaged area before any cleanup.", "Make temporary repairs only: tarp, board up, shut off water, dry out.", "Keep every receipt and note the hours you spent.", "Save samples of removed materials when possible.", "Ask the insurer in writing for its inspection date and keep the reply."], "close": "The days after damage feel like a race, and it's easy to fix the wrong thing first. Many homeowners lose part of their claim because the evidence is gone before anyone documents it, but you can protect both your home and your claim with the right first steps. Ready to call or text {{FIRST}} at {{PHONE}} so we can go over what to do before the adjuster arrives?"}, {"slug": "recoverable-depreciation", "title": "What Is Recoverable Depreciation and How Do I Get It Back?", "captures": "What is recoverable depreciation and how do I get it back?", "short": "On a replacement cost policy, the insurer often holds back depreciation from the first check and pays it later, once the repair or replacement is done. That held-back amount is called recoverable depreciation, and you usually get it by completing the work and sending proof, such as the final invoice, within the time your policy allows. Does your estimate show a line for depreciation being held back?", "body": ["Most homeowners are surprised when the first check is smaller than the estimate the insurer itself wrote. The missing money often isn't denied at all, it's held back as depreciation, but you have to know it's there and ask for it the right way to see it. Have you looked at the totals page of your insurance estimate?", "Depreciation is the insurer's way of accounting for the age and wear of what was damaged. On an actual cash value basis, they subtract it, but if you have replacement cost coverage, much of that amount is recoverable, meaning they pay it once you actually repair or replace the item. Do you know whether your policy is replacement cost or actual cash value?", "The estimate usually shows it plainly if you know where to look. You'll see a replacement cost total, a depreciation amount and an actual cash value total, and the first check is often based on the actual cash value minus your deductible, but the difference between the two is money waiting for you if it's marked recoverable. Can you find the word recoverable anywhere on your estimate?", "Getting it back takes paperwork. Most policies ask for proof the work was done, like a signed contract and a final invoice, and many set a deadline for completing repairs or submitting proof, but if you keep your contractor's paperwork organized, the request is usually straightforward. Has your contractor given you a written contract with a price?", "The trap is when the estimate itself is too low. If the insurer missed damage or underpriced the work, the depreciation they hold back is also too low, but a full, accurate estimate raises both what you're paid now and what you can recover later. Would you like a second set of eyes on your estimate before you start repairs?"], "quote": "Held-back money isn't denied money. You just have to claim it.", "next": ["Find the replacement cost, depreciation and actual cash value lines on your estimate.", "Check your policy for replacement cost coverage and any deadline to complete repairs.", "Get a written contract and keep the final invoice from your contractor.", "Send proof of completion to the insurer and keep a copy.", "Have a licensed public adjuster check whether the original estimate missed damage."], "close": "It's frustrating to fix your home and still feel like part of the money never came. Recoverable depreciation goes unclaimed more often than it should, but you can track it from the first estimate and get it back with the right paperwork. Ready to call or text {{FIRST}} at {{PHONE}} so we can find it on your estimate together?"}, {"slug": "roofer-handle-claim", "title": "Can My Roofer or Contractor Handle My Insurance Claim?", "captures": "Can't my roofer or general contractor just handle the insurance claim for me?", "short": "Your contractor can fix your home and give you a repair estimate, but in Florida a contractor generally can't adjust your claim, interpret your policy or negotiate with the insurer on your behalf unless they also hold a public adjuster license. A licensed public adjuster works for you on the claim itself, while your contractor focuses on the repairs. Has a contractor offered to deal with your insurance company for you?", "body": ["After a storm, contractors are often the first people at your door, and many offer to take care of the insurance side too. It sounds convenient when you're overwhelmed, but the person fixing your roof and the person representing your claim have different jobs, and Florida law treats them differently. Would it help to know where the line is?", "A contractor's job is to repair your home and price the work. Their estimate can be valuable evidence for your claim, but Florida law generally prohibits contractors from adjusting a claim, interpreting your policy or advising you about your coverage unless they hold a public adjuster license. Has anyone asked you to sign something that lets them talk to your insurer?", "There's also a conflict to think about. A contractor gets paid for the work they do, and their estimate is built around their own scope and prices, but the claim should cover everything the policy owes, including damage outside their trade, code upgrades and contents. Does your damage go beyond the roof or one part of the house?", "Be careful with any offer that sounds too generous. Florida law restricts contractors from offering to waive or rebate your deductible or offering gifts to get you to file a claim, so an offer like that is a red flag, but a good contractor will simply give you a fair written estimate and let you handle the claim with the right help. Did anyone offer to cover your deductible?", "The best setup is a team where everyone does their own job. A licensed public adjuster documents the full loss, reads your policy and negotiates with the insurer, while your contractor bids and does the repairs, but you stay in control of both relationships and the decisions. Would you like to talk about how that would work on your claim?"], "quote": "Your contractor fixes the house. Your public adjuster fights for the claim.", "next": ["Get a written repair estimate from your contractor.", "Don't sign anything that hands your claim or your insurance benefits to someone else without reading it carefully.", "Be wary of any offer to waive or pay your deductible.", "Verify any adjuster's license with the Florida Department of Financial Services.", "Keep your contractor and your claim paperwork in separate folders."], "close": "When you're tired and the house is a mess, letting one person handle everything is tempting. Many homeowners end up with a claim built around one trade's estimate and miss everything else, but you can have a contractor you trust and a licensed adjuster working for you at the same time. Ready to call or text {{FIRST}} at {{PHONE}} to talk about your claim before you sign anything?"}, {"slug": "claim-deadlines-florida", "title": "How Long Do I Have to File or Reopen a Claim in Florida?", "captures": "How long do I have to file or reopen a property insurance claim in Florida?", "short": "Under current Florida law, notice of a new or reopened property claim generally must be given to your insurer within one year of the date of loss, and a supplemental claim within 18 months. Your policy may set its own rules, and older losses may fall under earlier deadlines, so check the date of loss and your policy right away. Do you know the exact date the damage happened?", "body": ["Many homeowners think they have years to deal with a claim. Florida shortened its deadlines in recent years, and waiting can cost you the right to be paid at all, but knowing the dates up front gives you time to do it right. Do you know when your damage happened?", "For a new claim or a reopened claim, Florida law generally requires notice to the insurer within one year after the date of loss. That's a short window if the damage was hidden, like a slow leak, but reporting as soon as you find damage keeps your rights intact while you figure out the rest. Have you already reported the damage to your insurance company?", "A supplemental claim is for additional damage from the same loss that the insurer already adjusted. Florida generally allows notice of a supplemental claim within 18 months of the date of loss, which gives you time to find what was missed, but the clock starts on the date of loss, not the date you got your first check. Did the insurer's estimate leave out damage you can see now?", "Your policy matters too. Some policies have their own notice terms, and losses from before the law changed may follow older rules, but reading the conditions section of your policy tells you what you agreed to and what you need to send. Do you have a copy of your full policy, not just the declarations page?", "Deadlines are also the reason not to wait on a low payment. If you think the first check was too low, the time to document the missing damage is now, while the evidence is fresh and the 18 months are still running, but a written, organized supplement is much stronger than a phone call. Would you like help figuring out exactly where your deadlines fall?"], "quote": "The clock starts the day the damage happens, not the day you notice the check is short.", "next": ["Write down the date of loss and how you know it.", "Report the damage to your insurer in writing as soon as you find it.", "Mark one year and 18 months from the date of loss on your calendar.", "Request a full certified copy of your policy.", "Have a licensed public adjuster review any payment you think is too low before the window closes."], "close": "Losing a claim to a calendar is one of the most painful things that can happen to a homeowner. Deadlines in Florida are shorter than most people think, but you can protect your rights today just by knowing your dates and reporting in writing. Ready to call or text {{FIRST}} at {{PHONE}} so we can check your deadlines together?"}, {"slug": "reservation-of-rights-letter", "title": "What Does a Reservation of Rights Letter Mean?", "captures": "My insurance company sent me a reservation of rights letter. What does that mean?", "short": "A reservation of rights letter means the insurer is still looking at your claim but is keeping the option to deny some or all of it later based on a policy term it names. It is not a denial, but it tells you exactly which issue the insurer is worried about, so read it closely and respond in writing. Which policy section or issue does your letter mention?", "body": ["A letter with legal language from your insurance company can make your stomach drop. A reservation of rights sounds like bad news, but it's really the insurer saying it hasn't decided yet and telling you what it's concerned about, which gives you a chance to answer that concern. Have you read the whole letter, including the policy language it quotes?", "Insurers send these letters when they want to keep investigating without promising to pay. It protects the insurer from being told later that it gave up a defense, but it also puts the issue in writing, so you know exactly what to address, like late notice, wear and tear, or a question about how the damage happened. What reason does your letter give?", "The worst thing to do is ignore it. Insurers often ask for documents, a recorded statement or an inspection in the same letter, and missing those requests can hurt your claim, but answering promptly and in writing shows you're cooperating and builds your record. Does the letter ask you for anything by a certain date?", "Keep a copy of everything you send and receive. A photo of the letter, the envelope and the date it arrived is a good start, and a simple log of every call with the insurer helps, but sharing the letter with someone who reads these every day can tell you quickly how serious the issue is. Would it help to send a photo of the letter to a licensed public adjuster?", "Many reservation of rights letters end with the claim being paid. The insurer investigates, gets the information it needed, and moves forward, but the homeowners who come out best are the ones who respond clearly and keep the claim documented. Would you like help writing a response to your letter?"], "quote": "A reservation of rights is a question, not an answer. Answer it in writing.", "next": ["Read the whole letter and underline the policy section it quotes.", "Write down every deadline and request in the letter.", "Photograph the letter and envelope for your claim folder.", "Respond in writing and keep proof of when you sent it.", "Have a licensed public adjuster review the letter before any recorded statement."], "close": "Legal letters are designed to be careful, and they can leave a homeowner feeling like the claim is already lost. Most of the time it isn't, but how you respond in the next few weeks matters a lot. Ready to call or text {{FIRST}} at {{PHONE}}, or snap a picture of the letter, so we can look at it together?"}, {"slug": "claim-taking-too-long", "title": "My Insurance Claim Is Taking Forever. What Can I Do?", "captures": "My insurance claim is taking forever. What can I do?", "short": "Florida law sets timeframes for property claims: the insurer generally must acknowledge your claim within 7 days and pay or deny it, or the undisputed part of it, within 60 days of notice, with limited exceptions. If your claim is stalled, ask in writing for a status update and a copy of the adjuster's estimate, keep a log, and know that free state mediation is available for many disputes. How long ago did you report your claim?", "body": ["Waiting on an insurance company while you live in a damaged house wears you down. Some delays have real reasons, like a backlog after a big storm, but Florida law sets clear timeframes for insurers, and knowing them gives you something firm to point to. Do you know the date you first reported the claim?", "Florida generally requires your insurer to acknowledge a claim communication within 7 days and to pay or deny the claim, or the part of it they agree on, within 60 days after they receive notice. There are limited exceptions, like factors beyond the insurer's control, but a claim that sits for months with no answer is worth questioning. Has it been more than 60 days since you reported?", "Put your questions in writing. A phone call leaves no record, and adjusters change, but an email asking for the claim status, the adjuster's name and a copy of the detailed estimate creates a paper trail the insurer has to respond to. Have you received a copy of the insurer's estimate yet?", "Delays sometimes come from missing paperwork. Insurers can pause certain timeframes while they wait on information they asked you for, so a quick check of what they requested and when you sent it often finds the hold-up, but if you've sent everything, write that down and ask what else they need. Did the insurer ask you for documents or an inspection?", "If the claim is stuck or you disagree with the outcome, you have options. Florida offers free mediation through the Department of Financial Services for many residential property disputes, paid for by the insurer, and a licensed public adjuster can push the claim forward on your behalf. Would you like help figuring out which step makes sense for your claim?"], "quote": "A stalled claim moves faster when every request is in writing.", "next": ["Write down the date you first reported the claim.", "Email the insurer for a status update, the adjuster's name and the detailed estimate.", "Keep a log of every call, email and letter with dates.", "Check whether the insurer is waiting on anything from you.", "Ask about free state mediation, or have a licensed public adjuster step in."], "close": "Every week a claim drags on is another week living with damage. Delays are common, but they don't have to be endless, and a clear written record is what gets a stuck claim moving again. Ready to call or text {{FIRST}} at {{PHONE}} so we can look at where your claim stands?"}]};
