 tel='tel:+1'+d.slice(-10),wpa='<div class="sec wpa"><h2>What is a public adjuster?</h2><p class="big">A public adjuster is a licensed insurance professional you hire to handle your insurance claim. I\'m '+fn+', and I work for you, the property owner, not for the insurance company.</p>'
 +'<div class="tri"><div><b>01</b><h3>Read your policy</h3><p>I go through your coverage, endorsements and exclusions so you know what to ask for.</p></div><div><b>02</b><h3>Document the damage</h3><p>I inspect the property, photograph and measure the loss, list what was damaged, and build the repair estimate.</p></div><div><b>03</b><h3>Negotiate your claim</h3><p>I present your claim to the insurance company, answer what it asks for, and work toward a fair settlement for you.</p></div></div>'
 +'<h4>How a public adjuster is paid</h4><p class="pay">Most public adjusters charge a percentage of what the claim recovers, and some charge a flat or hourly fee. Fees vary by state and by adjuster, and the fee comes out of the settlement. Ask any adjuster to put the fee in writing before you sign anything.</p>'
 +'<h4>Who is who on your claim</h4><div class="who"><div><b>Insurance company adjuster</b><span>Works for the insurer and values the claim for the insurer.</span></div><div><b>Contractor</b><span>Repairs or rebuilds the damage. Usually cannot negotiate your claim for you.</span></div><div><b>Insurance attorney</b><span>Handles legal disputes and lawsuits.</span></div><div class="me"><b>Public adjuster</b><span>Works only for you on your claim. That\'s me.</span></div></div>'
 +'<div class="wcta"><p>Not sure your claim is big enough to need one? Call me, '+fn+', and we\'ll talk it through.</p><div class="cta"><a href="'+tel+'">&#128222; Call '+esc(fn)+'</a><a href="sms:+1'+d.slice(-10)+'">Text me</a></div><small>General information, not legal or insurance advice.</small></div></div>';
 var GUIDES=[["The first 48 hours after damage", ["Get everyone safe. If the house isn’t safe, leave it.", "Stop more damage if you can do it safely: tarp the roof, shut off the water, dry what you can.", "Take photos and video of everything before you clean up. Wide shots first, then close-ups.", "Keep damaged materials if you can. Don’t throw them out until the damage is documented.", "Save every receipt: tarps, fans, water removal, a hotel, meals.", "Report the claim to your insurance company and write down your claim number.", "Don’t sign anything you don’t understand."], "Not sure what to photograph? Call me and I’ll walk you through it."], ["Hurricane season checklist", ["Find your policy and save a copy on your phone and in your email.", "Know your hurricane deductible. It’s often a percentage of your home’s value, not a flat amount.", "Walk through your house and record a video of every room, opening closets and drawers.", "Photograph your roof, windows and the outside of the house from every side.", "Keep receipts for big purchases in one folder.", "Save my number in your phone now, before the storm."], "Want me to look at your policy before the season starts?"]];
